          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for chronic insomnia?",
          "answer": "According to the sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance summary (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line), cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia in adults, ahead of medication. The same document says its effects tend to last after treatment ends, while medication effects generally do not, and it avoids the harms linked to long-term sleep medicines.\n\nThe approach is usually delivered as a package over about four to eight sessions. Its components named in the passage include stimulus control (using the bed only for sleep and getting up when unable to sleep) and sleep restriction (limiting time in bed toward actual sleep time). The passage also notes that alcohol can make sleep worse even though people often use it as a sleep aid.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover how CBT-I is accessed, its full list of components, or what to do if it does not help. A clinician can talk through those options with you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
